<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke Zhuang of Zheng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 743–701 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duke Zhuang of Zheng (reigned 743–701 BCE) was a ruler of the State of Zheng who made it the leading state of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>. He defeated a revolt by his younger brother Gongshu Duan and quarrelled with the Zhou court, where he had served as a minister. In 707 BCE his army defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-huan-of-zhou">King Huan of Zhou</a> at Xuge and wounded the king, a blow to royal prestige.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duke-zhuang-of-zheng.jpg" alt="Duke Zhuang of Zheng" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 馮夢龍 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%84%AD%E8%8E%8A%E5%85%AC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Duke Zhuang was born in 757 BCE with the ancestral name Ji and the given name Wusheng, meaning difficult birth, a reference to a breech presentation. He was the elder of the two sons of Duke Wu of Zheng and was groomed for the throne. His mother, Wu Jiang, preferred his younger brother Duan, and repeatedly asked Duke Wu to make Duan heir, but she was refused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Wusheng became ruler of Zheng in 743 BCE. His mother asked that Duan be given the city of Zhi as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>; Zhuang refused but allowed any other city, and against his courtiers' advice he granted Jing, the second largest city of Zheng and an important fortress. Duan, urged on by his mother, built walls, stockpiled arms and recruited soldiers. Zhuang dismissed his ministers' warnings, saying he had no clear proof of a plot and could not take up arms against his brother.</p><p>While Zhuang was away in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> on diplomatic business, his mother wrote to Duan, ordering him to revolt and promising to open the gates of the capital. Zhuang, aware of the plan, returned in time to raise an army and defeated Duan at Yan, north of present-day Yanling County, in the summer of 722 BCE. A saying attributed to him about his brother runs that one who repeatedly commits undutiful acts will surely bring himself down.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-ping-of-zhou">King Ping of Zhou</a> appointed Zhuang Left Advisor, but King Huan removed him from office, and Zhuang refused to attend the royal court. Zhuang also led campaigns in the name of the Zhou king against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong people</a> and other Zhou states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zhuang died in 701 BCE, and his sons fought a long war of succession from 701 to 680 BCE. Of his 11 sons, Hu became Duke Zhao, but Duke Zhuang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> intervened and the minister Ji Zu installed Tu as Duke Li. Duke Zhao was later restored and then murdered by the vice minister Gao Qumi, who set up Men; Duke Xiang of Qi had Men and Gao Qumi killed, and Ji Zu made Yi duke. In 680 BCE Duke Li returned with Qi troops, defeated Yi and restored himself, and all later dukes of Zheng descended from him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duke Zhuang of Zheng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Duke Zhuang</b> made Zheng the leading state of the early Spring and Autumn period, and in 707 BCE his archers wounded King Huan of Zhou at Xuge, shattering royal prestige.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-zheng">State of Zheng</a></li></ul></section>
